    "aboveFold": true
   }
  },
  {
   "index": 11,
   "tag": "p",
   "landmarks": [
    "section",
    "main",
    "article",
    "section"
   ],
   "textLength": 364,
   "text": "The built-in WebPositive browser is now based on the WebKit rendering engine, which means it handles even demanding web apps such as Gmail perfectly. On Windows and Linux, Google's Blink dominates, but its ancestor WebKit is a major browser engine in its own right, with first-class support in almost all web apps – mainly thanks to a billion or so Apple iDevices.",
   "counts": {
    "links": 0,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 0,
    "listItems": 0,
    "tableRows": 0,
    "controls": 0,
    "paragraphs": 1,
    "repeats": 1,
    "coverLinks": 0
   },
   "linkTextRatio": 0,
   "geometry": {
    "top": 705,
    "height": 158,
    "widthFraction": 0.83,
    "leftFraction": 0.08,
    "pageFraction": 0.1,
    "aboveFold": true
   }
  },
  {
   "index": 12,
   "tag": "p×2",
   "landmarks": [
    "section",
    "main",
    "article",
    "section"
   ],
   "textLength": 847,
   "text": "If you want a big-name browser, though, there is a complete port of Firefox, with official Mozilla branding. What's more, the project isn't sticking to the slower-moving ESR version – since we installed beta 6, Firefox has been updated to the latest Firefox 155. You can also choose other Mozilla-family browsers, such as Waterfox, LibreWolf, or Floorp. GNOME Web is still there as well, but now that",
   "counts": {
    "links": 1,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 0,
    "listItems": 0,
    "tableRows": 0,
